==Satyr

=died1

#speaker:Athena
Well, what a surprise. Who could’ve guessed that a great fish philosopher would lose to a horned cheat? I can already see how all over Greece they will sing Odes about this

#speaker:Apistos
He just saw all my cards, that’s all.

#speaker:Athena
Uh-huh. Or maybe he read them right there in your… oh-so-honest, completely clueless fish eyes.

#speaker:Apistos
Thanks for the moral support, goddess.

#speaker:Athena
Always happy to remind you you’re hopeless.
->DONE

=died2
#speaker:Athena
And here you are again—the great and proud fish who just lost to a poor little satyr. I’m starting to doubt in your abilities. Need a little extra help?

#speaker:Apistos
I don’t need any help! That goat, literally a half goat, just distracted me, that’s all. Next time I’m gonna give that goat backside a real beating. And not just in Diodós!

#speaker:Athena
You seem to forget these games test your mind, not your muscle. Next time, be more careful… or cunning. Or you can play to the satyr a tune and maybe he’ll run away from your musical skills.

#speaker:Apistos
You don’t even know. I might be the best musician in all of Greece! Maybe even better than Apollo himself!

#speaker:Athena
I wouldn’t recommend challenging the god of the Sun and a handful of other talents. That could end with more than just a sunburn. Alright—less talk, more action. To the battle!
->DONE

=died3
